LIMITATIONS — this grammar is deliberately incomplete
-----------------------------------------------------
Upstream built it for dbt-extractor's static ref/source/config
extraction, so it parses only a small subset of Jinja:

Covered:
  - {{ expr }} where expr is a function call with LITERAL arguments
    (strings, ints/floats, True/False, lists, dicts with string keys),
    with keyword arguments
  - {% ... %} as ONE opaque token (jinja_expression) — no internal
    structure, no block nesting; '%}' inside a string ends it early
  - {# ... #} comments ({#- -#} included)

Missing relative to minijinja's tokenizer (the phase-1 pipeline):
  - {{- and -}} whitespace control
  - bare variables ({{ this }}, {{ x }})
  - attribute access / dotted calls (dbt_utils.star(...), adapter.x)
  - subscripts, filters (|), operators (~, arithmetic, comparisons,
    and/or/not, in, is), conditional expressions, tuples
  - lowercase true/false/none
  - raw blocks ({% raw %} contents are still scanned as Jinja)
  - identifiers as arguments (only literals parse)

Why this is acceptable here: crates/cogitator-treesitter uses the tree
only to SEGMENT the document (06 §3.3). Every Jinja region is tokenized
by minijinja afterwards; constructs outside the grammar surface as
tree-sitter ERROR nodes, and lib.rs routes any ERROR region containing
'{{', '{%' or '{#' through minijinja too. SQL between regions goes to
sqlparser (05 §4: no hand-written lexers).

Known quirk worked around in lib.rs: the grammar marks the comment-body
token HIDDEN, so Node::child() skips it and a sibling walk after '{#'
can land on a later construct — collect_regions therefore finds the
closing '#}' in the source text itself (regression-tested in
tests/behavior.rs).

Regenerating parser.c requires the tree-sitter CLI (ABI 14); no regen
script exists in this repository — patch grammar.js upstream-style and
re-vendor if grammar changes are ever needed.
